1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,336
A shipping clerk attaches a bill of lading
— a PDF — to a cargo booking. The browser
does not send JSON. It sends
multipart/form-data: fields plus a binary
part.

2
00:00:12,455 --> 00:00:23,664
If your controller still expects
@RequestBody, the adapter will not bind
what you think. Spring MVC treats
multipart as a first-class request shape
on the dispatcher path.

3
00:00:23,783 --> 00:00:36,863
Before handler invocation,
DispatcherServlet can resolve a multipart
request into a wrapped request the adapter
understands. Boot auto-configures a
MultipartResolver when servlet multipart
support is enabled.

4
00:00:36,983 --> 00:00:48,287
You bind parts with MultipartFile (or
Part) alongside ordinary form fields. Look
at this java for a moment. @RequestPart
names the multipart field.

5
00:00:48,407 --> 00:00:59,855
MultipartFile gives you bytes, size,
content type, and the client-supplied
filename. Treat that filename as hostile
metadata — never concatenate it straight
into a filesystem path.

6
00:00:59,975 --> 00:01:09,815
Prefer generated storage keys and store
the original name as data. Size limits are
production essentials, not afterthoughts:
Look at this example.

7
00:01:09,935 --> 00:01:18,287
spring: When a clerk uploads a 40MB scan,
the container should reject early with a
clear error rather than buffering until
the heap suffers.

8
00:01:18,407 --> 00:01:25,487
Align gateway limits, Boot multipart
limits, and reverse-proxy body sizes so
the failure mode is intentional.

9
00:01:25,607 --> 00:01:34,991
Streaming large files through getBytes()
is fine for small PDFs and wrong for
multi-hundred-megabyte transfers — use
getInputStream() and stream to object
storage.

10
00:01:35,111 --> 00:01:43,415
Virus scanning and content-type sniffing
belong in the store or a dedicated
pipeline; trusting Content-Type from the
client alone is not enough.

11
00:01:43,536 --> 00:01:51,359
On the dispatcher path, multipart
resolution happens before the adapter
invokes your method — remember the early
step in doDispatch.

12
00:01:51,480 --> 00:02:02,975
If resolution fails because the body is
not multipart or exceeds limits, your
controller never runs; advice and error
handling still should return a JSON
problem, not an HTML container page.

13
00:02:03,096 --> 00:02:12,288
People bind multipart with @RequestBody
and blame Jackson. Others disable size
limits "temporarily" for a demo and leave
them off.

14
00:02:12,408 --> 00:02:25,008
A third trap is writing uploads into a
directory inside the fat JAR’s working
tree without cleanup — disks fill, pods
restart, bookings look fine until storage
fails. Uploads now fit the MVC model.

15
00:02:25,128 --> 00:02:36,528
The broader craft around the stack you
already have — resource-oriented URLs,
disciplined statuses, paginated
collections, DTO boundaries, and
idempotent creates for parcels — is REST
best practice,

16
00:02:36,648 --> 00:02:39,096
not another annotation.
